    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away.** You should **negotiate**.\n\nYou hold significant leverage:\n1. Google recruited you passively.\n2. You are happy and established at Meta as an IC6.\n3. Tech compensation bands almost always have room above the opening offer for high-demand L6 PM talent.\n\nWhile Google\u2019s initial offer is solid and beats your Year 1/Year 2 cash flow due to Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule, it leaves money on the table for base salary and omits a sign-on bonus.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown vs. Current Meta Comp\n\n| Component | Current Meta (IC6) | Google Offer (L6) | Difference / Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Below current; max band is $271,000) |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** (Google L6 standard target is 20%) |\n| **Equity (Year 1)** | ~$200,000\u2013$250,000 | ~$356,400 | **+$106k\u2013$156k** (Google front-loads 33/33/22/12) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (Major opportunity area) |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **+$112,400 (+21%)** |\n| **4-Yr Average TC** | **~$526,000/yr** | **~$552,000/yr** | Flat grant average is $270k/yr equity |\n\n---\n\n### Key Dynamics to Understand\n\n#### 1. The Google Front-Loaded Vesting Schedule\nUnlike standard 4-year linear vesting (25%/yr), Google's new-hire grants follow a **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** schedule:\n* **Year 1:** $356,400 equity $\\rightarrow$ **$638,400 Total Comp**\n* **Year 2:** $356,400 equity $\\rightarrow$ **$638,400 Total Comp** (+ any Year 1 refresher)\n* **Year 3:** $237,600 equity $\\rightarrow$ **$519,600 Total Comp** (+ refreshers)\n* **Year 4:** $129,600 equity $\\rightarrow$ **$411,600 Total Comp** (+ refreshers)\n\nThis front-loading provides strong cash flow in Years 1 and 2, but creates an **equity cliff in Years 3 and 4**. While annual refreshers help cushion this, refresher sizes are performance-dependent and never guaranteed.\n\n#### 2. Base Salary is a Step Down\nYour base at Meta is $240,000. Google offered $235,000 against an SF range that tops out at $271,000. You should not take a base pay cut.\n\n#### 3. No Sign-On Bonus\nOpening offers for senior talent frequently omit a sign-on bonus to test candidate acceptance. A sign-on bonus is Google's most common mechanism to bridge transition risk, forfeited Meta bonuses, and unvested equity.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Proposal)\n\nAsk for the package to be adjusted to the top quartile of Google's L6 band:\n\n1. **Base Salary: $255,000**\n   * *Rationale:* Moves you above your current base ($240k) while remaining safely inside their posted SF range ($183k\u2013$271k).\n2. **Equity (GSUs): $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 over 4 years** (Up from $1.08M)\n   * *Rationale:* Brings Year 1 equity to ~$412k\u2013$429k, protecting you against Year 3/4 drop-offs.\n3. **Sign-on Bonus: $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Rationale:* Offsets leaving Meta mid-cycle and compensates for switching costs/unvested Meta equity.\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Script\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I've really enjoyed meeting the team, and I'm excited about the scope of the role. Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta, compensation is a critical factor for me to make a move.*\n> \n> *Looking at the numbers:*\n> 1. *The base salary of $235,000 represents a step down from my current $240,000 base. Given the posted range, I'd like to target **$255,000**.*\n> 2. *To offset forfeited equity/bonus timing at Meta and ensure I'm not taking a step back in later years with the 33/33/22/12 schedule, I'd like to see the equity grant at **$1.25M** and include a **$75,000 sign-on bonus**.*\n> \n> *If we can get the package to this level, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*",
